Z||000010||Butter||10-10-2001||04:11 PM||||87FieroGTx,<BR>You need to realize where the blunt of your wieght is and correct to it.  Problly the best idea on dirt is to back out of the gas and correct.  On dirt it depends what the gradiation of it is.  Some dirt is like ice (lose) and can range in compaction up to the point you can squeal tires on it (Packed hard).  The percent of moisture in it at any given point can also have a lot of bearing on the stability of it.  That packed hard dirt you could have squealed tires on in the evening after the dew sets on it can be like ice if it gets wet enough.  The amount of clay in the dirt also has a lot to do with it.  Tires are a big factor on dirt.  Usually dirt and street tires of any sort don't go well together.  <P>I like to ride my VW dune buggy (Rear engine) a lot and have a lot of fun sliding around in it like just after a short rain on dirt. Lotsa Fun were I enjoy doing most of my riding.  It ranges from sand to clay there and you can pick a spot and cut up!<P>Hope your girl friend learned a valuable lesson.  While I try to respect and whatch out for the wild life, one must remember not to get too radical in avoiding them if you all don't want to wind up a satistic.  I live in a rural area and have learned to whatch to see if wild life is on the move like at night when deer can become a big hazard.<P>Now what did you say you and your girlfriend were doing on a dirt road at night???  Racoons are nocternal animals ya know. Ha ha<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||CW85T-Type||10-10-2001||06:23 PM||||i've had alot of fun on dirt roads with my fiero.  When I watched my old bosses house, he lived 2 miles down a dirt road (twisty, hilly one) and I had a blast... going 50mph on dirt sliding through corners....  FUN!<P>||||reg||10
Z||000012||Primaris||10-10-2001||06:42 PM||||The biggest difference with driving on any loose surface is not to over correct.  Since it sounds like you just kept fish tailing from side to side you were overcorrecting.  If you have somewhere safe go out on dirt, gravel, snow fishtail the car.  When you go to straighten purposely input half of what you "feel" you should be doing.  Bet it snaps right back for ya.  Now the Fiero has a “no point of return” in a spin that is less than your typical car.  This happens to the Fiero because it is a mid engine design and not some flaw.<P>P.S. A Fiero is really hard to control at 55mph in 6+ inches of snow with worn Goodyear Eagles  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
